<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Phra That Lampang Luang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Phra That Lampang Luang is a Buddhist temple near Lampang in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, built within a walled enclosure on an old fortified mound. Its large gilded chedi, said to enshrine a relic of the Buddha, and its wooden open-sided halls make it one of the finest examples of Lanna architecture. Local tradition links it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nan-thip-chang">Nan Thip Chang</a>'s defeat of the Burmese governor of Lampang in 1732.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-phra-that-lampang-luang.jpg" alt="Wat Phra That Lampang Luang" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hong2101 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%87_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The temple was founded in the 15th century, though a myth gives it earlier beginnings, when the northern territories of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> formed the Hariphunchai Kingdom. According to a local legend, the Buddha passed through the area and the Lawa people offered him honey in a wooden tube. He threw the tube away, announced that the place would one day be known as Lampakappa Nakhon, and gave the people a single hair from his head, which they placed in a gold casket and buried in an underground tunnel with other valuables. Devotees believe this relic, with ashes from the Buddha's right forehead and neck, lies at the base of the chedi built over the spot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The complex is regarded as one of the best-preserved examples of Lanna architecture in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. The bell-shaped main chedi stands on a lotus-shaped base and is covered with bronze or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> sheets. The principal vihara, the Wiharn Luang, dates to the 15th century and has the open sides typical of Lanna temples, with three tiered roofs of wooden shingles on tall wooden columns, so that monks and laypeople can join ceremonies from inside or outside. Most buildings are of teak and local hardwood, with carved gables, doors and windows and interior pillars covered in gold lacquer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1732, after Burmese armies had taken Chiang Mai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a>, Burmese forces occupied the deserted city of Lampang and turned the temple into a camp. Thipchang, later known as Phraya Chaisongkhram, led his men into the temple in a surprise raid that defeated them, became ruler of Lampang and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chet-ton-dynasty">Chet Ton dynasty</a>, officially Thippachak. A statue of him stands in the complex, and bullet marks on the walls are taken as evidence of the fighting.</p><p>The temple is also known for an effect of light that works like a pinhole camera, projecting an image of the chedi. It can be seen clearly in two places: inside the vihara, and upside down within Ho Phra Phutthabaht, the pavilion of the Buddha footprint, which women may not enter under Lanna beliefs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wat Phra That Lampang Luang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lampang was a rice-growing principality on the Wang river with the revered <b>Wat Phra That Lampang Luang</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-lampang">Principality of Lampang</a></li></ul></section>
